r two (\\(M2\\)) is the lowest.\n- Major one (\\(M1\\)) is the second lowest.\n- Political (\\(P\\)) is the third lowest.\n- English (\\(E\\)) is the highest.\n\nTherefore, the correct ranking from high to low is:\n\\[\n\\text{Major subject two, major subject one, political, English}\n\\]\n\nThe correct option is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\] <answer>\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n<think>\nTo solve the logical reasoning problem, let's analyze the given conditions step by step and determine the correct ranking of Xiao Wang's four subjects from high to low.\n\n1. **Condition 1:** The sum of the scores of political and professional subject one is equal to the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   \\[\n   P + M1 = E + M2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Condition 2:** The sum of the scores of the political and professional subjects two is greater than the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   \\[\n   P + M2 > E + M1\n   \\]\n\n3. **Condition 3:** The scores of the professional subject one are higher than the sum of the scores of the political and English subjects.\n   \\[\n   M1 > P + E\n   \\]\n\nLet's analyze these conditions:\n\n- From Condition 3, we know \\( M1 > P + E \\).\n- From Condition 1, we have \\( P + M1 = E + M2 \\).\n- From Condition 2, we have \\( P + M2 > E + M1 \\).\n\nLet's substitute \\( P + M1 \\) from Condition 1 into Condition 2:\n\\[\nP + (E + M2) > E + M1\n\\]\nSimplifying this:\n\\[\nP + E + M2 > E + M1\n\\]\n\\[\nP + M2 > M1\n\\]\n\nWe already know from Condition 3 that \\( M1 > P + E \\). Combining this with \\( P + M2 > M1 \\), we get:\n\\[\nM2 > M1 > P + E\n\\]\n\nNow, let's use Condition 1 again:\n\\[\nP + M1 = E + M2\n\\]\nSince \\( M2 \\leq M1 \\), it follows that:\n\\[\nP + M1 \\leq E + M1\n\\]\n\\[\nP \\leq E\n\\]\n\nFrom Condition 3, we have:\n\\[\nM1 \\leq P + E\n\\]\n\nCombining these, we get:\n\\[\nM1 \\leq P + E \\leq E\n\\]\n\nThus, the order of the scores from highest to lowest is:\n\\[\nM2 \\leq M1 \\leq P + E \\leq E\n\\]\n\n### Ranking the Subjects:\n\n- Major two (\\(M2\\)) is the lowest.\n- Major one (\\(M1\\)) is the second lowest.\n- Political (\\(P\\)) is the third lowest.\n- English (\\(E\\)) is the highest.\n\nTherefore, the correct ranking from high to low is:\n\\[\n\\text{Major subject two, major subject one, political, English}\n\\]\n\nThe correct option is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\] <answer>\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n<answer>\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n<think>\nTo solve the logical reasoning problem, let's analyze the given conditions step by step and determine the correct ranking of Xiao Wang's four subjects from high to low.\n\n1. **Condition 1:** The sum of the scores of political and professional subject one is equal to the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   \\[\n   P + M1 = E + M2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Condition 2:** The sum of the scores of the political and professional subjects two is greater than the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   \\[\n   P + M2 > E + M1\n   \\]\n\n3. **Condition 3:** The scores of the professional subject one are higher than the sum of the scores of the political and English subjects.\n   \\[\n   M1 > P + E\n   \\]\n\nLet's analyze these conditions:\n\n- From Condition 3, we know \\( M1 > P + E \\).\n- From Condition 1, we have \\( P + M1 = E + M2 \\).\n- From Condition 2, we have \\( P + M2 > E + M1 \\).\n\nLet's substitute \\( P + M1 \\) from Condition 1 into Condition 2:\n\\[\nP + (E + M2) > E + M1\n\\]\nSimplifying this:\n\\[\nP + E + M2 > E + M1\n\\]\n\\[\nP + M2 > M1\n\\]\n\nWe already know from Condition 3 that \\( M1 > P + E \\). Combining this with \\( P + M2 > M1 \\), we get:\n\\[\nM2 > M1 > P + E\n\\]\n\nNow, let's use Condition 1 again:\n\\[\nP + M1 = E + M2\n\\]\nSince \\( M2 \\leq M1 \\), it follows that:\n\\[\nP + M1 \\leq E + M1\n\\]\n\\[\nP \\leq E\n\\]\n\nFrom Condition 3, we have:\n\\[\nM1 \\leq P + E\n\\]\n\nCombining these, we get:\n\\[\nM1 \\leq P + E \\leq E\n\\]\n\nThus, the order of the scores from highest to lowest is:\n\\[\nM2 \\leq M1 \\leq P + E \\leq E\n\\]\n\n### Ranking the Subjects:\n\n- Major two (\\(M2\\)) is the lowest.\n- Major one (\\(M1\\)) is the second lowest.\n- Political (\\(P\\)) is the third lowest.\n- English (\\(E\\)) is the highest.\n\nTherefore, the correct ranking from high to low is:\n\\[\n\\text{Major subject two, major subject one, political, English}\n\\]\n\nThe correct option is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\] <answer>\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n<answer>\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n<think>\nTo solve the logical reasoning problem, let's analyze the given conditions step by step and determine the correct ranking of Xiao Wang's four subjects from high to low.\n\n1. **Condition 1:** The sum of the scores of political and professional subject one is equal to the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   \\[\n   P + M1 = E + M2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Condition 2:** The sum of the scores of the political and professional subjects two is greater than the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   \\[\n   P + M2 > E + M1\n   \\]\n\n3. **Condition 3:** The scores of the professional subject one are higher than the sum of the scores of the political and English subjects.\n   \\[\n   M1 > P + E\n   \\]\n\nLet's analyze these conditions:\n\n- From Condition 3, we know \\( M1 > P + E \\).\n- From Condition 1, we have \\( P + M1 = E + M2 \\).\n- From Condition 2, we have \\( P + M2 > E + M1 \\).\n\nLet's substitute \\( P + M1 \\) from Condition 1 into Condition 2:\n\\[\nP + (E + M2) > E + M1\n\\]\nSimplifying this:\n\\[\nP + E + M2 > E + M1\n\\]\n\\[\nP + M2 > M1\n\\]\n\nWe already know from Condition 3 that \\( M1 > P + E \\). Combining this with \\( P + M2 > M1 \\), we get:\n\\[\nM2 > M1 > P + E\n\\]\n\nNow, let's use Condition 1 again:\n\\[\nP + M1 = E + M2\n\\]\nSince \\( M2 \\leq